 25ccee22\n\nNeither Vladimir Putin nor the war in Ukraine will last foreve
 r. What political order is likely to follow\, and what would it mean for R
 ussia\, Ukraine\, and the West?\n\nLeonid Gozman\, a longtime Russian oppo
 sition politician and political psychologist\, will examine the scenarios 
 that may unfold. A Ukrainian defeat\, he argues\, would likely produce a n
 ew war almost immediately. The end of Putin’s rule poses its own risks\,
  and the transition could prove more dangerous than the present. Yet Gozma
 n contends that a post-Putin Russia would probably be better for its citiz
 ens and the international community\, even if members of Putin’s circle 
 remain in power.\n\nThat transition\, he suggests\, may open a narrow wind
 ow for Russia to resume a path of normal political and economic developmen
 t. This discussion will consider what such a path would require\, what for
 ces could close it\, and what role domestic actors and Western governments
  might play in shaping the outcome.\n\nThe event will be moderated by Kath
 ryn Stoner\, Mosbacher Director of the Center on Democracy\, Development a
 nd the Rule of Law and Satre Family Senior Fellow at the Freeman Spogli In
 stitute for International Studies. Stanford affiliates are invited to atte
 nd in person\, and all others may join online via Zoom\; registration is r
 equired.\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nLeonid Gozman is the former president of 
 the Union of Right Forces and former co-chairman of the Right Cause Party 
